## Computers only work with numbers

Inside a computer, everything is a number: pictures, music, and text. So the first job of any
language model is to turn text into numbers. Type something below and watch each character turn
into its number.

<TextToNumbers />

## One number per character

<At levels="simple">

Every character has an agreed number, set by the Unicode Standard: capital *T* is 84, and a space
is 32. There are numbers for most of the world's writing systems, and for emoji too. Try typing *é*
or 🐱.<Cite id="unicode" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Every character has an agreed number, set by the Unicode Standard. Capital *T* is 84, a lowercase
*h* is 104, and a space is 32. Unicode has room for more than a million characters, covering most
of the world's writing systems, plus emoji.<Cite id="unicode" />

Try typing *é* or 🐱. They get numbers too, just bigger ones: *é* is 233 and 🐱 is 128,049.

</At>

## Bytes: how the numbers are stored

<At levels="simple">

Computers store those numbers in small pieces called **bytes**. One byte only holds a number up to
255, so bigger numbers take several bytes. Switch the widget to *UTF-8 bytes* to see it: *é* takes
two bytes, and 🐱 takes four. GPT-2 reads text as these bytes, so it can read any text at
all.<Cite id="radford2019" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Computers store numbers in **bytes**, and one byte can only hold a number from 0 to 255. A common
way to store text, called UTF-8, uses one byte for the basic English letters, digits, and
punctuation, and two to four bytes for everything else.<Cite id="rfc3629" /> Switch the
widget to *UTF-8 bytes* to see it: *é* becomes two bytes, and 🐱 becomes four.

GPT-2, the model used in this site, starts from these bytes. Its designers chose bytes so that the
model could read any text at all, in any language, without a special case for each
one.<Cite id="radford2019" />

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### How UTF-8 packs a number into bytes

Unicode numbers its characters from 0 to 1,114,111 (written U+0000 to U+10FFFF).<Cite id="unicode" />
UTF-8 picks how many bytes a character gets from its number $c$: one byte when $c < 128$, two when
$c < 2{,}048$, three when $c < 65{,}536$, and four for the rest.<Cite id="rfc3629" /> For a
two-byte character, the bytes are

$$
b_1 = 192 + \left\lfloor \frac{c}{64} \right\rfloor, \qquad b_2 = 128 + (c \bmod 64)
$$

where $\lfloor \cdot \rfloor$ rounds down and $c \bmod 64$ is the remainder after dividing by 64.
For *é*, $c = 233$: 233 is 3 sixty-fours with 41 left over, so the bytes are 195 and 169. Three-
and four-byte characters split $c$ the same way, six bits per byte after the first.

The scheme was built for two things. Text in plain English, with every character below 128, is
the same byte for byte as the older ASCII code. And every byte after the first in a character
starts with the bits 10 (a value from 128 to 191), so a reader dropped anywhere in a stream of
bytes can find where the next character starts.<Cite id="rfc3629" />

</At>

<RecapCard>

- Computers only work with numbers, so text must be turned into numbers first.
- Unicode gives every character a number, like 84 for *T*.
- UTF-8 stores those numbers as bytes: one for basic English letters, up to four for others.

<At levels="deep">

- UTF-8 uses one byte below 128, two below 2,048, three below 65,536, and four for the rest.

</At>
</RecapCard>

